Assurance Degradation Dynamics (ADD)

A Research Proposal for Trajectory-Based Frontier AI Safety Assurance

Version 0.4

ouadi Maakoul 

AI : chatGpt, Deepseek, Meta AI 

Abstract

This proposal investigates whether a trajectory-based formalization of assurance degradation can complement existing frontier-AI safety-case and monitoring methodologies. Assurance Degradation Dynamics (ADD) is presented as a research hypothesis and formalization program—not as an established safety methodology. Unlike static safety cases, ADD models the evolution of assurance quality as a dynamical system coupled to capability growth during training. We define a normalized assurance state space, a log-derivative divergence operator, and an assurance debt variable with explicit repayment dynamics. Following critical review of v0.3, this version corrects several mathematical errors and gaps: (i) the state-space identifiability problem is resolved by computing assurance quality and debt deterministically from filtered estimates of observable states; (ii) the functional selection criterion is tightened to preserve positivity and conjunctive composition; (iii) Lemma 1 is labeled as a definitional consequence; (iv) Proposition 2 is corrected to an upper-bound statement; (v) Proposition 3 is restricted to stationary-variance windows; (vi) the filter is specified as an Unscented Kalman Filter to handle nonlinearity in the assurance-quality functional; (vii) strategic observation is modeled via an adversarial innovation-monitoring layer. Empirical grounding is updated with public datasets and a pre-registered retrospective protocol. This framework does not measure safety; it measures the quality and trajectory of our knowledge about safety.

---

1. Problem Statement

1.1 The Capability–Assurance Divergence

Frontier AI development exhibits a structural asymmetry: capability metrics improve monotonically with scale and compute, while assurance mechanisms—evaluation validity, monitorability, and safety-case confidence—do not necessarily improve at the same rate. The ICLR 2026 position paper on AI safety debt formalizes this as "the cost of closing the accumulated gaps between an AI system's actual safety approach and the approach it needs," identifying four structural properties that make such debt harder to manage than software technical debt: capabilities and contexts shift unpredictably, closing gaps may require solving open scientific problems, harms largely fall on third parties, and adversaries and AI systems may actively exploit gaps.

EvalAwareBench provides controlled evidence that evaluation awareness varies by model–benchmark pairing, that recognition rarely leads to behavioral change, and that when it does, the direction depends on the type of evaluation perceived. This complicates any monotonic mapping from awareness to degradation.

1.2 The Gap in Existing Formalisms

Existing frameworks are predominantly snapshot-based. Dynamic safety cases have been proposed for frontier AI, emphasizing monitorability, assessability, and updateability. Subjective Logic-based confidence updates integrate windowed Safety Performance Indicators into assurance arguments. Control barrier functions provide provable safety guarantees for dynamical systems, but are designed for control-theoretic safety, not epistemic assurance.

No existing formalism treats assurance quality as a dynamical system with a debt-accumulation state variable and covariance-based structural early warning in a training-time monitoring architecture for frontier AI.

1.3 Formal Problem Statement

Given a frontier training run \mathcal{T} = \{t_0, t_1, \ldots, t_N\} with observable capability signals C(t) and assurance signals \mathbf{A}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^k, determine whether the trajectory (C(t), \mathbf{A}(t)) exhibits assurance degradation: a state in which the rate of capability growth exceeds the rate of assurance quality improvement such that the system enters a region of irreducible epistemic uncertainty about its own safety properties.

