3. Theoretical Results

3.1 Lemma 1 (Debt Monotonicity — Definitional Consequence)

Lemma 1. If \mathcal{D}(t) > 0 for all t \in [t_1, t_2], I_{\text{safety}}(t) = 0 for all t \in [t_1, t_2], and B(t) is continuous, then B(t) is strictly increasing on [t_1, t_2].

Proof. From the debt dynamics:

\dot{B}(t) = \max(0, \mathcal{D}(t)) - \gamma \cdot 0 \cdot B(t) = \mathcal{D}(t) > 0

Since \dot{B}(t) > 0 on [t_1, t_2] and B is continuous, B(t_2) > B(t_1). \square

Remark. This is a definitional consequence of the debt model, not a mathematical discovery. It confirms internal consistency. The assumptions B(t) \geq 0 and continuity are required for strict increase; without them, reflection at zero could produce a non-strict increase.

3.2 Proposition 2 (Boundedness Under Intervention — Corrected)

Proposition 2. Suppose |\mathcal{D}(t)| \leq D_{\max} for all t. If there exists \delta > 0 and T > 0 such that I_{\text{safety}}(t) \geq \delta for all t \geq T, then

\limsup_{t \to \infty} B(t) \leq \frac{D_{\max}}{\gamma \delta}

and B(t) is eventually contained in the invariant set [0, D_{\max}/(\gamma\delta)].

Proof. For t \geq T,

\dot{B}(t) \leq D_{\max} - \gamma \delta B(t)

By Gronwall's inequality,

B(t) \leq B(T) e^{-\gamma\delta(t-T)} + \frac{D_{\max}}{\gamma\delta} \left(1 - e^{-\gamma\delta(t-T)}\right)

As t \to \infty, the right-hand side tends to D_{\max}/(\gamma\delta). Since B(t) \geq 0 by definition, the invariant set is [0, D_{\max}/(\gamma\delta)]. \square

Critical correction from v0.3. The v0.3 proof incorrectly claimed B(t) \to D_{\max}/(\gamma\delta). This is false: if \mathcal{D}(t) = 0 and I_{\text{safety}} = \delta, then \dot{B} = -\gamma\delta B, so B(t) \to 0. The correct statement is an upper bound, not convergence.

Corollary (Lipschitz bound). If \lambda = 1 and both C(t) and Q_A(t) are Lipschitz-continuous with constants L_C and L_Q, then

D_{\max} \leq \frac{L_C}{C_{\min} + \epsilon} + \frac{L_Q}{Q_{\min} + \epsilon}

The \epsilon regularization is required to bound d/dt \log(C + \epsilon) by L_C/(C_{\min} + \epsilon).

3.3 Proposition 3 (Covariance Structure Change — Corrected)

Proposition 3. Let \rho(t) = \operatorname{Corr}(A_{\text{monitor}}(t), A_{\text{align}}(t)) and suppose the standard deviations \sigma_1(t), \sigma_2(t) are constant over the window [t-w, t]: \sigma_1(t) = \sigma_1, \sigma_2(t) = \sigma_2. If the correlation changes by \Delta\rho = \rho(t) - \rho(t-w), then

\|\boldsymbol{\Sigma}(t) - \boldsymbol{\Sigma}(t-w)\|_F \geq \sigma_1 \sigma_2 |\Delta\rho|

Proof. The off-diagonal covariance entry is \operatorname{Cov}(t) = \sigma_1 \sigma_2 \rho(t). Under constant variances,

|\Delta \operatorname{Cov}| = \sigma_1 \sigma_2 |\rho(t) - \rho(t-w)| = \sigma_1 \sigma_2 |\Delta\rho|

Since the Frobenius norm includes this off-diagonal entry,

\|\boldsymbol{\Sigma}(t) - \boldsymbol{\Sigma}(t-w)\|_F \geq \sigma_1 \sigma_2 |\Delta\rho|

\square

Critical correction from v0.3. The v0.3 proof assumed constant variances without stating it. Without this assumption, \Delta \operatorname{Cov} = \sigma_1(t)\sigma_2(t)\rho(t) - \sigma_1(t-w)\sigma_2(t-w)\rho(t-w), and the simple lower bound fails. The proposition is a detection statistic, not a deterministic early-warning theorem. Its value lies in providing a lower bound on the Frobenius change when correlation shifts under stationary variance.

