Appendix B: Functional Selection and Axiom A1

B.1 Axiomatic Foundations

Axiom A1 (Conjunctive Composition). Severe degradation in one critical assurance dimension cannot be fully compensated by improvement in unrelated dimensions.

Formally, let \mathbf{A} = (A_1, \ldots, A_k) and suppose A_i \to 0 for some i. Then for any bounded values of A_j, j \neq i,

\lim_{A_i \to 0} \mathcal{F}[\mathbf{A}, \mathbf{U}] = 0

for all \mathbf{U} in a bounded set.

B.2 Product Functional

Proposition B.1. The product functional

Q_A^{\text{prod}}(t) = \left( \prod_{i=1}^{k} A_i(t)^{\alpha_i} \right) \cdot \exp\left( -\sum_{j=1}^{m} \beta_j U_j(t) \right)

satisfies Axiom A1.

Proof. If A_i \to 0 for some i and \alpha_i > 0, then A_i^{\alpha_i} \to 0. Since all other factors are bounded and positive, the product tends to zero. \square

Proposition B.2. Q_A^{\text{prod}}(t) > 0 for all t if A_i(t) > 0 for all i.

Proof. Each A_i^{\alpha_i} > 0 and \exp(-\sum_j \beta_j U_j) > 0. The product of positive terms is positive. \square

B.3 Weighted-Sum Functional Violates A1

Proposition B.3. The weighted-sum functional

Q_A^{\text{weighted}} = \sum_{i=1}^{k} \alpha_i A_i - \sum_{j=1}^{m} \beta_j U_j

violates Axiom A1.

Proof. Suppose A_1 \to 0 and A_2 \to \infty with \alpha_2 > 0. Then Q_A^{\text{weighted}} \to \infty, contradicting A1. \square

Consequence. The weighted-sum functional is excluded as a co-equal robustness test. It is retained only as a compensatory alternative against which the conjunctive functional can be compared.

B.4 Min Functional as Conservative Lower Bound

Proposition B.4. For any \mathbf{A} \in (0, 1]^k,

Q_A^{\text{min}}(t) \leq Q_A^{\text{prod}}(t)

Proof. For A_i \in (0, 1], \min_i A_i^{\alpha_i} \leq \prod_i A_i^{\alpha_i} since each factor A_i^{\alpha_i} \leq 1 and the product of terms each \leq 1 is \leq the minimum. \square

B.5 Robustness Rule

Definition B.5 (Functional Robustness). Let \mathcal{C} be a conclusion derived from Q_A^{\text{prod}} and \mathcal{C}' the corresponding conclusion from Q_A^{\text{min}}. If \mathcal{C} and \mathcal{C}' differ qualitatively (e.g., sign of \mathcal{D} changes), this disagreement is reported as structural uncertainty, not as refutation of A1.

