Appendix D: Unscented Kalman Filter Implementation

D.1 Sigma Point Generation

For state dimension n = 1+k+m, generate 2n+1 sigma points:

\mathcal{X}_0(t-1) = \hat{\mathbf{x}}(t-1|t-1)

\mathcal{X}_i(t-1) = \hat{\mathbf{x}}(t-1|t-1) + \left(\sqrt{(n+\lambda)\mathbf{P}(t-1|t-1)}\right)_i, \quad i = 1, \ldots, n

\mathcal{X}_{i+n}(t-1) = \hat{\mathbf{x}}(t-1|t-1) - \left(\sqrt{(n+\lambda)\mathbf{P}(t-1|t-1)}\right)_i

where \lambda = \alpha^2(n+\kappa) - n is the scaling parameter, \alpha \in (0, 1] controls the spread of sigma points, and \kappa is a secondary scaling parameter.

D.2 Weights

W_0^{(m)} = \frac{\lambda}{n+\lambda}, \quad W_0^{(c)} = \frac{\lambda}{n+\lambda} + (1 - \alpha^2 + \beta)

W_i^{(m)} = W_i^{(c)} = \frac{1}{2(n+\lambda)}, \quad i = 1, \ldots, 2n

where \beta incorporates prior knowledge of the distribution (for Gaussian, \beta = 2).

D.3 Prediction Step

\mathcal{X}_i(t|t-1) = f(\mathcal{X}_i(t-1))

\hat{\mathbf{x}}(t|t-1) = \sum_{i=0}^{2n} W_i^{(m)} \mathcal{X}_i(t|t-1)

\mathbf{P}(t|t-1) = \sum_{i=0}^{2n} W_i^{(c)} \left(\mathcal{X}_i(t|t-1) - \hat{\mathbf{x}}(t|t-1)\right)\left(\mathcal{X}_i(t|t-1) - \hat{\mathbf{x}}(t|t-1)\right)^{\top} + \mathbf{Q}

D.4 Update Step

\mathcal{Z}_i = h(\mathcal{X}_i(t|t-1))

\hat{\mathbf{z}}(t|t-1) = \sum_{i=0}^{2n} W_i^{(m)} \mathcal{Z}_i

\mathbf{S}(t) = \sum_{i=0}^{2n} W_i^{(c)} \left(\mathcal{Z}_i - \hat{\mathbf{z}}(t|t-1)\right)\left(\mathcal{Z}_i - \hat{\mathbf{z}}(t|t-1)\right)^{\top} + \mathbf{R}

\mathbf{P}_{xz}(t) = \sum_{i=0}^{2n} W_i^{(c)} \left(\mathcal{X}_i(t|t-1) - \hat{\mathbf{x}}(t|t-1)\right)\left(\mathcal{Z}_i - \hat{\mathbf{z}}(t|t-1)\right)^{\top}

\mathbf{K}(t) = \mathbf{P}_{xz}(t) \mathbf{S}(t)^{-1}

\hat{\mathbf{x}}(t|t) = \hat{\mathbf{x}}(t|t-1) + \mathbf{K}(t)(\mathbf{z}(t) - \hat{\mathbf{z}}(t|t-1))

\mathbf{P}(t|t) = \mathbf{P}(t|t-1) - \mathbf{K}(t) \mathbf{S}(t) \mathbf{K}(t)^{\top}

D.5 Innovation Monitoring

The innovation sequence is

\boldsymbol{\nu}(t) = \mathbf{z}(t) - \hat{\mathbf{z}}(t|t-1)

Under Gaussian assumptions, \boldsymbol{\nu}(t) is white with covariance \mathbf{S}(t). Test whiteness via the time-averaged autocorrelation function:

\hat{\rho}_k = \frac{\sum_{t=k+1}^{N} \nu_t \nu_{t-k}}{\sum_{t=1}^{N} \nu_t^2}

Flag STRATEGIC_OBSERVATION if |\hat{\rho}_k| exceeds the 95% confidence limit for any lag k.

