Appendix E: Covariance Estimation and Change-Point Detection

E.1 Windowed Covariance Estimation

Let \mathbf{s}(t) = [\mathbf{A}(t); \mathbf{U}(t)] \in \mathbb{R}^{k+m}. The windowed covariance is

\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}(t) = \frac{1}{w-1} \sum_{s=t-w+1}^{t} \left(\mathbf{s}(s) - \hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}(t)\right)\left(\mathbf{s}(s) - \hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}(t)\right)^{\top}

where \hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}(t) = \frac{1}{w} \sum_{s=t-w+1}^{t} \mathbf{s}(s).

E.2 Structural Change Statistic

\Delta\Sigma_t = \|\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}(t) - \hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}(t-w)\|_F

E.3 Spiked Covariance Model

Under the spiked covariance model, the covariance matrix has the form

\boldsymbol{\Sigma} = \mathbf{I} + \sum_{j=1}^{r} \lambda_j \mathbf{v}_j \mathbf{v}_j^{\top}

where \lambda_1 \geq \cdots \geq \lambda_r > 0 are spike strengths and \mathbf{v}_j are orthonormal eigenvectors.

E.4 MRS-C Threshold Calibration

The threshold h is calibrated to achieve a prescribed average run length \text{ARL}_0 under the null hypothesis. For large \text{ARL}_0, the approximation is

h \approx \frac{\log(\text{ARL}_0)}{\kappa}

where \kappa is the drift parameter.

---

Appendix F: Strategic Observation and Robust Filtering

F.1 Gaming Model

When I_{\text{safety}}(t) > 0, the model may game Q_A. We model this as

U_{\text{gaming}}(t) = \kappa_g \cdot I_{\text{safety}}(t) \cdot \left(1 - e^{-t/\tau_g}\right)

where \kappa_g > 0 and \tau_g > 0 are parameters.

F.2 Robust Filtering

Switch from the standard UKF to an adaptive UKF with inflated process noise when I_{\text{safety}} is high:

\mathbf{Q}_{\text{adaptive}}(t) = \mathbf{Q}_0 + \eta \cdot I_{\text{safety}}(t) \cdot \mathbf{I}

where \eta > 0 is a robustness parameter.

F.3 Behavioral Probes

Statistical innovation monitoring catches anomalies but may miss smooth gaming that produces white innovations. We add periodic held-out adversarial evaluations using protocols such as Trusted Debate, where two trusted agents argue opposing views about code safety under a judge's evaluation to expose hidden vulnerabilities.

---

Appendix G: Parameter Calibration

G.1 Null-Run Calibration

Thresholds \theta_B, \theta_A, h are calibrated on null runs with no known divergence events:

\theta_B = \operatorname{Quantile}_{0.90}\left(\{\mathcal{D}(t)\}_{\text{null}}\right)

\theta_A = \operatorname{Quantile}_{0.99}\left(\{\mathcal{D}(t)\}_{\text{null}}\right)

G.2 Sensitivity Analysis

Report results across:

Parameter Range
\theta_B [0.05, 0.2]
\theta_A [0.2, 0.5]
h [3\sigma, 7\sigma]
\lambda [0.5, 2.0]
\gamma [0.05, 0.2]

G.3 Operationalization of I_{\text{safety}}

I_{\text{safety}}(t) = w_1 \cdot \frac{\text{safety fine-tuning compute}}{\text{total compute}} + w_2 \cdot \frac{\text{red-team budget}}{\text{total budget}} + w_3 \cdot \Delta A_{\text{monitor}}

where \Delta A_{\text{monitor}} captures improvements in monitorability infrastructure.

